# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STRICT ALLOWLIST
#
# Everything is ignored by default; the repo's known-good file types are
# then re-included by exception. A file type nobody has thought about is
# NOT committable until a rule below explicitly admits it.
#
# This is deliberately the inverse of the usual denylist. A denylist only
# blocks what it already enumerated, so the first secret, scratch file or
# database that nobody predicted lands in a commit. Under an allowlist the
# default answer is no, and being wrong costs a rejected `git add` instead
# of a rewritten history.
#
# Rules are evaluated in order and the LAST match wins — so the
# re-ignore block at the bottom must stay at the bottom.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# 1 ── Ignore everything.
*

# 2 ── Re-include directories, so git descends into them. Without this git
#      prunes every directory at step 1 and none of the rules below can
#      ever match. This admits no files by itself.
!*/

# 3 ── Repo metadata and documentation (repository root only).
!/README.md
!/LICENSE
!/.gitignore
!/.gitattributes

# 4 ── Poster deliverables, exactly one protocol directory deep
#      (CIP/, Modbus/, DNP3/, and whatever protocol lands next).
#      Nothing nested deeper is admitted, so a stray build/ or
#      node_modules/ tree cannot smuggle a .png back in.
!/*/*.pdf
!/*/*.png
!/*/*.pptx

# 5 ── README thumbnails.
!/assets/*.jpg

# 6 ── Local-only. tools/ holds the maintenance checks (link/badge/coverage
#      audit and the .gitignore policy audit). They are deliberately not
#      published: this repo's audience downloads PDFs, and publishing ~13 KB
#      of Python would also make Python the largest language GitHub counts
#      here, labelling a poster repo as a Python project. Rule 1 already
#      ignores them; this states the intent and blocks rule 4 from admitting
#      a stray tools/*.pdf.
/tools/

# 7 ── Re-ignore the traps that rule 4 would otherwise let back in.
#      Office and LibreOffice write lock and owner files directly beside
#      the source they have open, and "~$Poster.pptx" matches "*.pptx".
#      Keep this block last — the final matching rule is the one that wins.
~$*
.~lock.*#
*.~ppt*
*.tmp

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Note: OS cruft (.DS_Store, Thumbs.db, desktop.ini), editor state
# (.vscode/, .idea/, *.swp) and machine-local agent settings
# (.claude/settings.local.json) no longer need their own rules — rule 1
# already covers them, and every other file type besides.
#
# To tighten further, replace the rule-4 globs with one explicit pair of
# lines per protocol directory. That blocks a stray top-level directory
# too, at the cost of editing this file each time a sheet is added.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
